# Line endings and binary detection for the whole repository.
#
# Why this file exists. Every platform here reads files another platform wrote:
# the .sh packaging scripts under build/ and native/ are run on Linux and macOS
# out of the same checkout Windows uses, and the .ps1 / .wxs / .wixproj side is
# the reverse. `core.autocrlf` is a per-machine setting and cannot express that
# — a contributor with it turned off commits CRLF once and 13 `#!/bin/sh`
# scripts start failing on Linux with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M". A
# repository-level declaration applies to everyone, on every clone, with no
# setup. The rules below are the contract; do not relax one to suit a local
# editor.

# Default: let git decide text vs binary, normalise to LF in the object store.
* text=auto

# --- Must keep LF on every platform ---------------------------------------
# A CRLF in any of these is not a style question, it is a broken file: the
# kernel reads the shebang line literally, and launchd / systemd / the XDG
# desktop parser are no more forgiving. This also covers the WSL, Docker
# bind-mount and copy-to-a-Linux-box workflows, where the checkout is read
# directly by the other OS.
*.sh                text eol=lf
native/macos/native-dependencies.lock text eol=lf
*.bash              text eol=lf
Dockerfile*         text eol=lf

# Debian control files and maintainer scripts carry no extension of their own,
# so they have to be named individually. Same for the macOS package's postinstall,
# which the kernel (or launchd) reads literally.
#
# The systemd environment file used to be named here too, as build/service/linux/default.
# It is remotedesktop-server.default now -- named after the /etc/default/remotedesktop-server
# it is installed as, rather than after the directory it happens to sit in -- so the glob
# below covers it and anything else given the same shape.
build/installer/linux/**/control                             text eol=lf
build/installer/linux/**/postinst                            text eol=lf
build/installer/linux/**/postrm                              text eol=lf
build/installer/linux/**/config                              text eol=lf
build/installer/linux/**/templates                           text eol=lf
build/installer/linux/**/*.txt                               text eol=lf
build/installer/macos/**/*.txt                               text eol=lf
build/installer/macos/**/scripts/postinstall                 text eol=lf
build/installer/macos/**/scripts/preinstall                  text eol=lf
*.default                                                    text eol=lf

*.plist             text eol=lf
*.service           text eol=lf
*.desktop           text eol=lf

# Workflow files: their run: blocks are shell, and keeping them LF means the
# file reads the same whichever OS a maintainer opens it on.
*.yml               text eol=lf
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md text eol=lf
build/docker/.env.example                              text eol=lf

# --- Windows tooling and project files ------------------------------------
# PowerShell accepts LF, but CRLF is what every Windows editor and the WiX
# toolset produce; declaring it keeps a round trip through one of those from
# showing up as a whole-file diff.
*.ps1               text eol=crlf
*.psm1              text eol=crlf
*.cmd               text eol=crlf
*.bat               text eol=crlf
*.wxs               text eol=crlf
*.wixproj           text eol=crlf
*.slnx              text eol=crlf
*.csproj            text eol=crlf
*.props             text eol=crlf
*.rtf               text eol=crlf

# The Windows copy of the server quick guide is generated with CRLF by
# build/tools/Export-ServerConfigurationGuides.ps1, and the release job's -Check compares
# the file byte for byte. Without this line the guide is checked out LF on Linux (the blob
# is LF) while the generator writes CRLF, so `-Check` passed on the Windows job and failed
# in check-version — the one job that gates a release.
build/installer/windows/**/*.txt    text eol=crlf

# --- Binary assets ---------------------------------------------------------
# Never diff, never convert. git would detect most of these on its own; saying
# so keeps a future file that happens to look like text from being mangled.
*.png               binary
*.ico               binary
*.icns              binary
*.dylib             binary
*.so                binary
*.dll               binary
*.exe               binary
*.pdb               binary
*.snk               binary
*.pfx               binary
*.nupkg             binary
*.zip               binary
